<td width=3D"514" height=3D"309">
<h1 align=3D"center">Sinking Object</h1>
<p>For the description of an object sinking from a rest position, both the =
<a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/http://hyperphysics.p=
hy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/pbuoy.html#buoy">buoyant force</a> and <a href=3D"htt=
ps://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.ed=
u/hbase/airfri.html#c3">viscous resistance</a> must be included.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td>
<img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hyperphysi=
cs.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf2b.gif">
<h3 align=3D"center"><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/=
http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/lindrg.html#c3">Motion equations=
</a></h3></td>
<td>

 <center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://h=
yperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3b.gif"></center><p>U=
sing the net gravitational acceleration</p>
<center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hy=
perphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3c.gif"></center>
<p>yields terminal velocity</p>
<center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hy=
perphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3d.gif"></center>
<p>and characteristic time</p>
<center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hy=
perphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3e.gif">.</center>
</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The velocity at time t can be stated in terms of the terminal velocity a=
nd the characteristic time.</p>
<center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hy=
perphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3f.gif"></center>

<p>The distance traveled in time t is </p>
<center><img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727im_/http://hy=
perphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/fluids/imgflu/viscf3g.gif"></center>
<p>This motion can be modeled for a spherical object using the expression f=
or <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/http://hyperphysic=
s.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/airfri.html#c3">viscous resistance</a> in terms of=
 the fluid <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/http://hyp=
erphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/pfric.html#vis">viscosity</a>. </p>




<form method=3D"" action=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080208110727/http=
://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/lindrg.html">
=20
<p>Consider a sphere of mass m =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"mg" value=
=3D"" size=3D"6"> gm =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"m" value=3D"" size=3D=
"6"> kg</p>
<p>and density &#961; =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"dg" value=3D"" size=
=3D"6"> gm/cm<sup>3</sup> =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"d" value=3D"" si=
ze=3D"6"> kg/m<sup>3</sup></p>
<p>corresponding to radius r =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"rc" value=3D"=
" size=3D"6"> cm =3D <input type=3D"text" name=3D"r" value=3D"" size=3D"6">=
 m</p>
<p>If such a sphere sinks through a fluid of density <input type=3D"text" n=
ame=3D"dfg" value=3D"" size=3D"6">gm/cm<sup>3</sup> =3D <input type=3D"text=
" name=3D"df" value=3D"" size=3D"6"> kg/m<sup>3</sup></p>
<p>the initial gravitational acceleration will be g' =3D <input type=3D"tex=
t" name=3D"gp" value=3D"" size=3D"6"> m/s<sup>2</sup> =3D <input type=3D"te=
xt" name=3D"gpf" value=3D"" size=3D"6"> g.</p>
